 "Performance side by side: HET against the IFO on the same surface": {
  "table:1": {
   "y": 1.3
  },
  "txt:The one-step rows sit 0.5 % low for HET on both rigs — and that is the estimator, not the reading: a synthetic 1.000 nm poke reads at slope 1.000000 at the pixel level; the LO placement, the vector sum, the polarizers and the mask each cleared; at λ_m = 10⁻⁵ the gain is 1.0007.  It is the one-step Tikhonov shrinkage at the record's λ_m = 10⁻³ — the \"matrix bias\" every gauge carries, now a number.": {
   "y": 6.15,
   "fs": 12
  }
 },
 "Electronics: what each gauge needs behind the optics": {
  "txt:Camera timings are TO's records on an IMX250MZR-class camera (2448 × 2048, 75 fps; well ~10 ke⁻ and read 2.4 e⁻ unconfirmed; the IFO's four analyzer frames in one exposure; S and P on the mono variant at 18.8 readings/s).  The sensors are 10–40× slower than photons ÷ full-sensor rate would say: the ZWFS image's bright core sets the exposure (dynamic range), the frame mean sits at half well, and two polarization orientations go unused — the IFO's uniform pupil does not pay those.  Parts counts and TRL to follow (campaign brief sec. 9.1).": {
   "x": 0.5,
   "y": 6.8
  }
 },
 "COPHI against the Zernike sensor with a dedicated laser, for the hold job": {
  "txt:For hold alone, the vector Zernike sensor is the cheapest in photons by ~50× and needs no stabilities of its own — but on the assumed polarization camera (10 ke⁻ well) it is the *slowest* in time, ~60 s per 3 pm cycle; Feng's camera makes COPHI slow (16 s cycles); the Photonfocus makes it fast (1.6 s); the lock-in makes COPHI the fastest reading on the table (0.125 s cycles at 3.09 pm, 3.35 s per pm; records) — if its well behaves as surveyed — and its fiber term is a per-sample piston, inert (record).": {
   "y": 5.75,
   "fs": 11
  }
 }
}